Your analysis seems to be correct.  In my naivete, I thought Win98 would
leave lots of free memory in a 128Meg of RAM machine.  Not true.  By the
time all the crud gets sucked in at startup, there's only 12.2 Meg of
physical memory free to use to start NetHack.  There's plenty of swap
memory, but I'm guessing you can't do the needed malloc out of swap, but
must do it out of physical memory _before_ it can swap. The bitmap file
needs to contain 19960800 bytes of data, not counting container
overhead.

I restarted Win98 in "Safe Mode", which leaves out all but the
essentials, and was able to open NetHack using the Absurd64.bmp tileset
without errors, which I have reported to the DevTeam.  I don't know if
that leaves a fix possible for systems with this little memory.

